From 723819bf67b16690c0e44d4310cad681d2346211 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 01:40:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] bluez5: allow preferring a codec sample rate per device,
 at runtime

The A2DP sample rate is fixed when the codec configuration is selected,
not per stream, and the rate that gets preferred there could so far only
be set once, monitor-wide, via the bluez5.default.rate property. Picking
a different rate for one device meant editing the config and
reconnecting it.

Give spa_bt_device its own preferred rate, which overrides the
monitor-wide default when a codec configuration is selected for that
device. It can be set as a device property, and it is exposed on the
device Props param, so it can also be changed at runtime:

  pw-cli set-param <id> Props '{ params = [ "bluez5.default.rate", 44100 ] }'

Changing it renegotiates the codec configuration of a connected device,
which tears the transport down and sets it up again: there is a short
audible gap, unavoidably, as the rate is a property of the transport.

BlueZ-initiated connections select their configuration through
endpoint_select_configuration(), which cannot tell which device the call
belongs to and therefore cannot honour a per-device preference. So also
check, when ensuring a codec, whether the configuration an existing
transport carries is still the one that would be selected now, and
renegotiate if not. The check compares configuration bytes rather than
the rate itself, so a preference the remote cannot satisfy settles after
one renegotiation instead of being retried forever, and it is skipped
entirely for devices without a preference of their own, so behaviour is
unchanged unless the property is set.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
